[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Distributed Document Routing and Lifecycle in Elasticsearch"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Elasticsearch is a distributed search and analytics engine designed to handle massive volumes of data. Understanding how documents are routed across nodes and managed throughout their lifecycle is crucial for building scalable and performant search architectures."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"How Distributed Document Routing Works"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you index a document in Elasticsearch, it does not just go anywhere. Elasticsearch uses a precise hashing algorithm to determine which primary shard the document belongs to. This process relies on the document ID."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"shard_num = hash(routing) % primary_shards_count"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, the routing value is the document ID. However, you can explicitly define a custom routing value to ensure that related documents are stored on the same shard, which optimizes query performance for parent-child relationships or multi-tenant architectures."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"PUT /my_index/_doc/1?routing=user_123
{
  "title": "Document Routing Example",
  "user": "user_123"
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"The Document Lifecycle"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Managing indices as they grow, age, and eventually get deleted is handled by Index Lifecycle Management (ILM). ILM automates the transition of indices through four distinct phases:"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hot: The index is actively being written to and queried."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Warm: The index is no longer receiving writes, but is still being queried."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cold: The index is rarely queried and can be moved to cheaper hardware."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Delete: The index is no longer needed and is safely removed from the cluster."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing an ILM Policy"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can define an ILM policy using the Kibana console or the Elasticsearch API. Below is an example of a policy that rolls over the index when it reaches 50GB or is 30 days old, and deletes it after 90 days."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"PUT _ilm/policy/logs_policy
{
  "policy": {
    "phases": {
      "hot": {
        "actions": {
          "rollover": {
            "max_size": "50GB",
            "max_age": "30d"
          }
        }
      },
      "delete": {
        "min_age": "90d",
        "actions": {
          "delete": {}
        }
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining efficient document routing with automated lifecycle management, you ensure optimal resource utilization, predictable query latency, and sustainable storage costs over time."}]
